Service managers|A service manager supervises processes and coordinates their lifecycle with system dependencies.|systemd represents services and other resources as units. Start order, readiness and restart policies are separate settings. A process running does not necessarily mean its application is ready to serve traffic.|systemctl status ssh.service
Enable versus start|Starting a service activates it now; enabling arranges activation through configured boot or target relationships.|A service can be enabled but stopped, or running but disabled. Check both states when diagnosing reboot-only failures. Some static units are activated by dependencies or sockets and are not directly enabled.|systemctl is-enabled ssh.service
Unit overrides|Unit drop-ins customize packaged systemd units without editing vendor files.|The manager combines the base unit and overrides. After editing, daemon-reload refreshes unit definitions; a service restart or reload may still be needed to apply runtime changes. Use systemctl cat to inspect the effective sources.|systemctl cat ssh.service
Journals|The systemd journal collects structured service and system log entries.|Filter by unit, boot and time range to align evidence with an incident. Retention and persistent storage settings determine what survives reboot. Empty results can reflect filtering or retention rather than absence of a failure.|journalctl -b -p warning --no-pager
Accounts|System accounts bind numeric user and group identities to ownership and authorization rules.|Services should use dedicated identities with limited access. Directory permissions, supplementary groups and login policies affect what those identities can do. Removing an account does not automatically reassign every file owned by its old UID.|getent passwd
SSH keys|SSH public-key authentication proves possession of a private key without sending that key to the server.|The server checks authorized keys and policy; the client separately verifies the server's host identity. Restrict key access and rotate compromised credentials. A trusted user key does not remove the need to verify the host key.|ssh-keygen -lf ~/.ssh/id_ed25519.pub
Package management|A package manager installs versioned software and tracks dependencies and package-owned files.|Use trusted repositories and understand distribution-specific update behavior. Installation success does not prove running processes use patched code. Plan service restarts and verify versions and health after maintenance.|dpkg-query -W openssh-server
Scheduled jobs|Schedulers execute recurring or delayed tasks through cron, timers or other job systems.|Jobs often run with a different environment and working directory from an interactive shell. Define absolute paths, output handling, locking and timeout behavior. Test overlapping runs and missed-run semantics rather than relying on the calendar expression alone.|systemctl list-timers --all
Time synchronization|Clock synchronization aligns system wall time with trusted time sources.|Accurate time supports certificate validation, logs and distributed coordination. Inspect source selection and offset rather than only whether the daemon runs. Applications should still use monotonic clocks for elapsed durations.|timedatectl status
Disk layout|Disk layout describes devices, partitions, logical volumes, filesystems and their mount points.|These layers have separate sizes and identities. Expanding a cloud disk may still require partition, logical-volume and filesystem expansion. Use stable identifiers and confirm the exact target before modifying storage.|lsblk -o NAME,SIZE,FSTYPE,MOUNTPOINTS
Log rotation|Log rotation bounds log-file growth by renaming, compressing or expiring older data.|Applications may need a signal or reopen operation after rotation because open descriptors continue referencing old files. Copy-truncate has race and data-loss trade-offs. Match retention to capacity and investigation needs.|logrotate --debug /etc/logrotate.conf
Backups|A backup preserves a recoverable copy of data and required metadata outside the active failure domain.|Choose schedules and retention from recovery objectives. Protect encryption keys and credentials separately from the failing system. Replication alone is insufficient when it quickly copies deletion, corruption or malicious changes.|
Restore verification|Restore verification tests that a backup can produce a complete and usable system.|Restore into an isolated target, validate integrity and execute representative application reads or transactions. Record data age and elapsed recovery time. A checksum validates bytes but does not prove application consistency or dependency availability.|
RAID|RAID combines disks to provide selected performance or device-failure tolerance properties.|Different levels have different capacity and failure trade-offs. Rebuilds increase load and may expose additional disk failures. RAID does not protect against accidental deletion, application corruption or loss of the entire system.|cat /proc/mdstat
LVM|Logical Volume Manager pools block devices and allocates logical volumes from volume groups.|Physical volumes supply extents; logical volumes consume them and may host filesystems. Extending a logical volume and growing its filesystem are distinct steps. Shrinking support and order depend on the filesystem and need careful planning.|lvs
Filesystem mounts|Mounting makes a filesystem accessible at a path in the directory tree.|Persistent definitions commonly use UUIDs in fstab or mount units. A missing mount can cause an application to write into the underlying root filesystem directory. Confirm source, options and dependencies before starting storage-dependent services.|findmnt
Boot recovery|Boot recovery diagnoses failures in firmware, bootloader, kernel, initramfs, mounts or service startup.|Locate the failing stage and use the distribution's rescue workflow. A bad fstab entry and a missing storage driver require different fixes. Preserve console access and a known-good boot option before changing critical configuration.|journalctl -b -1 -p err
Patching|Patching updates software to address defects or vulnerabilities under a controlled rollout.|Identify affected running components, test compatibility and define rollback or forward recovery. Apply to a small cohort first, then verify service behavior before broader rollout. Reboot requirements depend on the changed components and update mechanism.|
Configuration drift|Configuration drift is divergence between intended configuration and actual system state.|Compare effective settings, package versions and manual changes with the declared baseline. Decide whether an emergency change should be adopted or reverted. Blindly restoring a baseline during an incident can undo a necessary mitigation.|
Access reviews|Access reviews check that current privileges still match current responsibilities.|Include local groups, sudo rules, SSH keys, service identities and emergency access. Remove stale grants through a controlled process and verify a supported administration path remains. An inventory of accounts alone misses delegated privileges.|
Sudo policy|Sudo policy determines which users may execute which commands as another identity.|Command paths, arguments and environment handling influence the scope of a grant. An allowed shell or editable script may effectively grant unrestricted privilege. Validate policy syntax before installing it and inspect effective rights.|sudo -l
Resource limits|Resource limits bound process or service consumption such as descriptors, tasks, memory and CPU.|Shell limits and service-manager settings can differ. Inspect the limits on the actual running process, not just the login shell. Raise a limit only after distinguishing legitimate demand from leaks or unbounded concurrency.|cat /proc/1/limits
System monitoring|System monitoring measures host health, saturation and service behavior over time.|Correlate CPU, memory, storage, network and application signals during the same interval. A current healthy snapshot may miss an earlier spike. Use baselines and historical trends to separate expected workload from regression.|vmstat 1 5
Immutable hosts|Immutable infrastructure replaces hosts from a defined image instead of accumulating in-place changes.|Store durable data outside replaceable instances and automate bootstrapping. An image version makes rollback clearer, but external schemas and state may still require migration compatibility. Emergency edits should be captured in the image definition.|
Capacity forecasts|Capacity forecasting estimates when demand will exceed usable resource headroom.|Use growth trends, seasonality, procurement delay and failure reserve. Track the actual bottleneck rather than extrapolating CPU alone. Revisit forecasts after major workload or architecture changes.|
Remote administration|Remote administration provides controlled access to systems without local console interaction.|Maintain a tested emergency path, protect authentication and confirm the target before changes. For network or SSH changes, preserve an existing session until a separate new login succeeds. Otherwise a small configuration error can lock out responders.|
Data sanitization|Data sanitization makes retired storage data unrecoverable to the required assurance level.|Methods depend on medium, encryption design and threat model. File deletion or formatting is not equivalent to secure erasure, especially with snapshots and SSD wear leveling. Track backup copies and key disposal as part of retirement.|
Fleet changes|Fleet changes apply a configuration or software update across multiple machines.|Use canary groups, bounded batches and health gates. Separate failure domains so one bad change does not remove all capacity at once. Stop automatically when error thresholds or health checks fail.|
Disaster documentation|Disaster documentation records how to restore a service when normal systems or personnel are unavailable.|Include dependency order, recovery locations, credential retrieval and validation steps. Keep an accessible protected copy outside the affected environment. Test whether someone unfamiliar with the system can follow it.|
Change verification|Change verification confirms both the intended result and continued service health.|Compare pre-change and post-change evidence, including user-facing behavior and dependency metrics. A zero exit code only confirms a command's own success conditions. Keep rollback criteria explicit until the observation window passes.|
